ction following according to human evaluations, while explicitly noting that the resulting models still made simple mistakes. Instruction following therefore contributes controllability but does not establish factual correctness. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155?utm_source=chatgpt.com) Yao et al. (2022/2023) interleaved language-model reasoning traces with task-specific actions, enabling models to update plans and obtain information from external sources. Their evidence comes from QA, fact-verification and interactive benchmarks, not the present marketing application. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.03629?utm_source=chatgpt.com) Schick et al. (2023) demonstrated a model trained to determine which external APIs to call, when to call them, what arguments to provide and how to incorporate returned information. This supports a technical tool-use capability; it does not establish organizational permission, correct tool selection in an unseen workflow, or safe autonomous authority. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761) The resulting capability lineage remains: transformer-based language processing -> scaled in-context/few-shot task adaptation -> retrieval of external information -> improved instruction following -> interleaved reasoning/action -> API/tool invocation. The inherited capabilities remain flexible language processing, retrieval-grounded generation, instruction-conditioned behavior and bounded multi-step information/tool interaction. The inherited limitations also remain: generated language can be wrong; retrieval does not establish source applicability; instruction following does not establish factual correctness; and technical action capability does not confer permission, judgment or reliable autonomous authority. Nothing in this creation history proves diffusion, present market maturity, organizational readiness, compliance, ROI, enterprise value or safe autonomous authority. The historical need/opportunities represented in the papers must not